1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:23,190
Esteban said the wrong man's name, and Cassius stood up so fast his
chair fell over. Nero Chrome, Esteban said. That was my father. I know
whose father he was. Nigga, I said, everybody leave the potatoes out
of this. Cassius wasn't looking at me. He had one hand flat on the
table and the other held against his injured shoulder.

2
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:47,394
Esteban sat very straight, his wife's picture beneath his palm. Inez
stood between them before either man moved again. Papa, you don't know
everything. I know who was beside the car. You know what Bellario told
you. Cassius laughed once. No amusement in it. Of course. Bellario. He
turned toward the courtyard gate. I caught him there. Where you going?

3
00:00:47,644 --> 00:01:09,553
Somewhere my father isn't being tried by a man who let the duke buy
his mouth. Esteban's chair scraped behind us. Tank put one hand on its
back. Sit down, my nigga. I will not— Then stand still. Pick one. I
kept my eyes on Cassius. You can hear him and still hate what he says.
I've heard it for seven years.

4
00:01:09,803 --> 00:01:34,019
Then hear it once with me here. His jaw moved. He looked at Shawna,
who had come to stand beside the table, holding Amalia's photograph
carefully by the edges. Your father drove this car? she asked. Cassius
looked back despite himself. The White Heron, he said. He built it
with Esteban. Then sit down and tell us something nobody else gets
right. That reached him.

5
00:01:34,269 --> 00:01:58,349
He picked his chair up. He didn't apologize, and Esteban didn't ask
him to. Sometimes, nigga, the best you can get from two men is both
their asses back at the table. Esteban began again. Seven years
earlier, the Grand Prix had been smaller. There were rich men in it,
but money wasn't the only invitation. Mechanics entered cars they had
built behind their houses.

6
00:01:58,599 --> 00:02:21,671
Boat captains raced old engines down the coast. The drivers ate at the
same tables as the people who changed their tires, and at the end of
the month the winner sailed to Isla Gloria with everybody who had
helped him get there. Amalia loved the last night, Esteban said. She
would dance until the musicians begged her to stop. Rosa smiled
faintly.

7
00:02:21,921 --> 00:02:43,228
I know that kind of woman. She collected people. If a waiter looked
lonely, suddenly he was sitting with us. If a driver lost, she found
something he had done well and made him talk about that. When Aurelio
bought the race, she told him the prize was already here. He only
needed to leave it alone. What'd he say? I asked.

8
00:02:43,478 --> 00:03:00,478
That a woman who enjoyed giving things away should marry a poorer man.
Nigga. Esteban nodded. He said it smiling. That year Esteban and Nero
had entered the White Heron together. One drove the mountain stages,
the other the coastal ones.

9
00:03:00,728 --> 00:03:25,285
Amalia kept their books, knew every worker by name and had discovered
that the prize fleet included vessels the duke had never actually paid
for. Their owners had been promised shares in the Grand Prix. Instead,
crews stopped receiving wages, captains disappeared from the entry
list and the harbor gates began closing at night. She went to the
island, Esteban said.

10
00:03:25,534 --> 00:03:46,433
A captain named Celadon took her. She came back with a packet of
papers and told me we had to finish the race. Win it in front of
everybody. Then we could make him give back what he had taken. Why
didn't she show you the papers? Shawna asked. She did. I looked at the
first page, kissed her forehead and said I trusted her.

11
00:03:46,683 --> 00:04:06,146
His voice roughened. I thought that was the same as listening. I felt
Shawna's thumb move against my hand. Esteban told us about the final
mountain stage. The White Heron led by eleven seconds. Nero had driven
the morning. Esteban was due to take over at the ridge.

12
00:04:06,396 --> 00:04:28,897
Amalia traveled ahead in a little blue support car carrying the papers
and the team's food, because even when a man was about to expose a
duke she thought he ought to have lunch. At the ridge, Nero never
arrived. Instead, Bellario brought news that the support car had gone
over the road. Amalia was dead. Nero had vanished.

13
00:04:29,147 --> 00:04:52,614
A green harbor car had been seen leaving the scene. The duke showed me
a signed withdrawal, Esteban said. Nero's name. Money paid. A place on
a ship leaving that night. Bellario said he watched Nero get into the
green car. Cassius leaned forward. My father couldn't write with his
right hand that week. He'd broken two fingers. Esteban blinked.

14
00:04:52,864 --> 00:05:12,417
The signature was steady as a schoolmaster's, Cassius said. I told
them. I was twenty-four, and I told every man in that room. They said
grief was making me irrational. Why didn't you tell me? You wouldn't
open your gate. The words landed harder than shouting would have.
Esteban stared at him.

15
00:05:12,667 --> 00:05:35,552
Inez looked away, and I understood this was a conversation they had
been walking around for seven years, keeping it alive by never
allowing it to finish. Cassius took something out of his jacket. A
little metal bird, white paint worn off one wing. My father gave me
this the morning of the race. Said if he won, I'd drive the first lap
next year.

16
00:05:35,802 --> 00:06:01,953
He set it beside Amalia's photograph. He never came home. Outside the
courtyard a car horn sounded. Nobody moved. It sounded again, and Tank
stood up with the expression of a man grateful for a fresh target. He
opened the gate. Bellario stood beyond it beside a cream-colored
limousine, dressed for dinner, feather restored. His Grace requests
Captain Aiden's presence at the opening banquet.

17
00:06:02,203 --> 00:06:24,749
I looked at the nigga for a long time. Does he? Attendance is
traditional. So is knocking. Bellario glanced past Tank and saw the
table. Saw Cassius, Esteban, Inez. His face didn't change, but his
eyes counted us twice. An intimate gathering, he said. Getting less
intimate. Shawna squeezed my hand and stood. We'll be there.

18
00:06:24,999 --> 00:06:44,434
Bellario bowed to her with the precise depth of a man who practiced
alone. His Grace particularly hopes you will sing. Then he should
particularly hope he's paid my musicians. The bow ended early. After
he left, Esteban went inside and returned with a small lacquer box.

19
00:06:44,684 --> 00:07:07,180
He opened it to reveal a folded letter, a yellow ribbon and a
photograph of Amalia laughing beside a white boat. This came three
days after she died, he said. A woman delivered it. She wouldn't give
her name. I read the first line so many times I almost wore through
it. What's it say? I asked. He unfolded the top edge.

20
00:07:07,430 --> 00:07:29,147
Esteban, if you are reading this without me, do not let a man who
never loved either of us explain my last day. Cassius closed his eyes.
Esteban held the letter toward Inez. I wasn't brave enough to obey
her. She took it without a word. We left the table standing. Plates,
bread, photograph, the little white bird.

21
00:07:29,397 --> 00:07:51,018
Nobody tried to make it look like a dinner that had ended normally. At
the hotel, Shawna changed into a blue dress that moved around her legs
like water had learned her name. She wore Dante's watch on her wrist
and earrings I had given her after Sky High. She stood before the
mirror fastening one, and I came up behind her.

22
00:07:51,268 --> 00:08:12,297
You want to skip this? I asked. No. We can eat somewhere nobody named
Cruel owns the forks. She met my eyes in the mirror. I want to sing. I
want Rosa to get paid. I want that nigga to look at us across his own
table while we find out what he's hiding. That's my wife. She turned
around.

23
00:08:12,547 --> 00:08:33,449
And you want to race him. I want to beat him. Then do it with your
eyes open. I kissed her forehead. She pushed my chest lightly. Don't
Esteban me. Nigga, I laughed so hard she smiled before she could help
it. Then I took her face in both hands. I hear you. You want to sing,
Rosa gets paid, we watch the duke.

24
00:08:33,699 --> 00:08:51,672
I don't go running off because somebody waves a faster car. And? And
if it's a really fast car, I bring you. She hit me with the earring
box. The banquet occupied a terrace above the sea. Three hundred
people sat beneath chandeliers hanging from living trees.

25
00:08:51,922 --> 00:09:14,137
At the entrance, a young doorman looked at my invitation, then at my
badge, then back at the invitation. "It says king." "It also says
captain," I told him. "Which should I announce?" Tank leaned over his
shoulder. "Nigga, use your diaphragm and say both." "Is there a
preferred order?" "The nigga arrived first at everything today.

26
00:09:14,387 --> 00:09:38,172
Any order works." The doorman laughed, took a breath and announced me
so loudly the nearest chandelier trembled. I slipped a folded bill
into his palm. "That's for respecting punctuation." He looked at the
amount, looked at me and announced my wife with even more feeling.
Nigga had found a second diaphragm. A string orchestra played on a
platform over the swimming pool.

27
00:09:38,422 --> 00:10:01,163
Along the terrace stood twelve cars under silk covers, each with its
own guard and a woman holding a silver card naming the race it
belonged to. I walked in with Shawna on my arm, my badge against my
chest and Tank behind me in a white suit that made him look like he
had personally purchased summer. Conversations stopped in waves.

28
00:10:01,413 --> 00:10:18,855
The first wave was people recognizing me. The second was people asking
the first wave who I was. The third was everybody looking at Shawna
and deciding the answer must be more impressive than they had heard.
The duke rose from a long table at the center.

29
00:10:19,105 --> 00:10:42,631
Aurelio Cruel was forty-eight, tall, broad through the shoulders,
handsome in the way a sword can be handsome before somebody pulls it
out. His hair was black with two white streaks. He wore a green
diamond on one hand and a watch whose face seemed larger than the gold
wheel I'd won. Captain, he said. Your entrance this afternoon was
costly. For who?

30
00:10:42,881 --> 00:11:01,378
The people close enough to hear laughed before they looked at him. He
let them finish. For the harbor's dignity. Nigga, the harbor selling
shirts. This time the laughter reached the next table. He smiled.
Come. Sit beside me. There were two chairs waiting. One for me, one
for Shawna.

31
00:11:01,628 --> 00:11:25,633
Farther down the terrace, Mercedes and Porsche had been directed
toward a little table behind a pillar. I stopped. Where's my people?
We've arranged seating by distinction. Then you arranged it wrong. The
duke rested his fingertips on the back of my chair. A captain. A
celebrated singer. Naturally, some guests have qualifications others
do not. Mercedes had heard him.

32
00:11:25,883 --> 00:11:45,817
She walked over before I could answer, yellow silk catching the light.
Mine got you to call my agency three times last year, she said.
Porsche's got your minister to pay double for dinner and still ask if
she had a sister. Porsche appeared beside her. I told him no. He
looked financially devastated.

33
00:11:46,067 --> 00:12:06,531
A man at the duke's table suddenly became fascinated by his soup. The
duke's smile thinned. There is no need for vulgarity. Nigga, you
invited me, I said. I took the chair beside him, lifted it and carried
it to Mercedes's table. Tank picked up the table itself. Rosa brought
the flowers.

34
00:12:06,781 --> 00:12:29,721
Within twenty seconds we had joined two tables together directly in
front of the orchestra, and half the terrace was standing to help
because nobody wanted to be the last person on the wrong side of the
evening. Cassius arrived with Inez. He saw what we were doing and
dragged his own chair over one-handed. Careful, I told him. It's my
good arm. Keep one.

35
00:12:29,971 --> 00:12:51,067
The duke stood alone beside his empty place settings. Then he laughed.
It was a good laugh. Full, generous, almost convincing. A king makes
his own table, he said. I sat down and pulled Shawna into the chair
beside me. Now you catching on. The waiter approached with a bottle
and looked at the duke, then at me.

36
00:12:51,317 --> 00:13:13,215
"Who should taste?" "That nigga owns the bottle," Tank said. "This
nigga owns the table." "I own neither," I said. "My wife wanted wine.
Give the woman wine." Shawna held out her glass. "These niggas will
appoint a president of the bottle if you let them." Mercedes nodded.
"Nigga will run unopposed." "And win," I said.

37
00:13:13,465 --> 00:13:36,187
The waiter laughed so suddenly he had to steady the bottle with both
hands. The duke didn't laugh at all. Nigga was losing authority to a
drink he had paid for. Dinner came. Lobster, lamb, fish served whole
on silver platters. Tank looked at the fish and said, This better not
be the nigga you saved. Rosa nearly dropped her wine.

38
00:13:36,437 --> 00:13:53,877
The duke joined us after the second course. He had accepted that if he
wanted the most important table at his party, he would have to sit at
mine. He told stories about princes losing fortunes. He described a
driver who had bet his castle and left in a taxi.

39
00:13:54,127 --> 00:14:18,087
He named the prize fleet: forty road cars, twelve racing cars, six
yachts and a flagship called the Sovereign Sun. At the end of the
month, the winner would sail that flagship into Isla Gloria's harbor
and receive the island before every camera in the world. And if you
lose? I asked. I do not compete. Convenient. I own the competition.
For now.

40
00:14:18,337 --> 00:14:41,269
He watched me over his glass. Shawna's hand settled on my knee. The
orchestra quieted. Rosa rose first, went to the little stage and sang
a song so rich and warm the sea itself seemed to lean closer. Tank sat
still through the whole thing. Nigga, the man could lift a table with
dinner on it, but that woman's voice pinned him to a chair.

41
00:14:41,519 --> 00:15:01,857
When she finished, he stood up before anybody else. That's Rosa! he
shouted. Everybody knew. He told them anyway. Then Shawna joined her.
My wife began without an introduction. One clear note, then another,
rising over the water. I had heard her sing in rooms where I was the
only person listening.

42
00:15:02,107 --> 00:15:21,657
I'd heard her angry, laughing, half asleep, making up words because
she couldn't remember the real ones. This was different. She stood in
the light with her shoulders bare and let the whole terrace hear how
large she was inside. The duke stopped drinking. A woman at the next
table began to cry.

43
00:15:21,907 --> 00:15:40,490
I sat forward, both hands flat on my knees, because if I leaned back I
felt like my chest might open. Rosa answered her. Their voices wound
together. The orchestra followed, and for once in my life, nigga, I
didn't need to be doing the most impressive thing in the room.

44
00:15:40,740 --> 00:16:04,654
My wife was doing it, and she kept looking at me like the song had an
address. When they finished, the terrace stood. Shawna came back
through hands reaching for hers. A conductor kissed her fingers. One
old man said something in Italian so emotional his wife nodded like he
had finally gotten a point right after forty years. The duke rose to
greet her.

45
00:16:04,904 --> 00:16:26,015
You must perform on Isla Gloria, he said. The opera house was built
for a voice like yours. Shawna smiled. Then make sure the people who
clean it get paid. His hand stopped halfway toward hers. At the edge
of the terrace, a waiter dropped a glass. Bellario was beside him
instantly. Too quickly.

46
00:16:26,265 --> 00:16:49,023
He caught the man's arm and began steering him toward a service door.
The waiter looked back once, directly at our table. Lark stood up.
That man knows Mateo. I rose with her. The duke stayed between us and
the door. Captain, dessert— Nigga, move. His eyes hardened. Then he
did. By the time we reached the corridor, Bellario was gone.

47
00:16:49,273 --> 00:17:13,157
The waiter stood alone beside a linen cart, shaking, blood on one
finger where the glass had cut him. Mercedes took a clean napkin and
wrapped his hand. What's your name? she asked. Luc Marel. How old are
you? Twenty-nine. Okay, Luc. Breathe. He looked past us toward the
terrace. They said if I spoke to the drivers— Tank shut the corridor
door.

48
00:17:13,407 --> 00:17:39,093
Now you're speaking to us. Luc swallowed. Then he pulled a folded
service menu from his pocket. On the back, in hurried pencil, was a
list of names. Thirty-two of them. Mateo was halfway down. Beside the
list were two words. CINDER DOCK. Below that, a time. MIDNIGHT FRIDAY.
Lark covered her mouth with one hand. Luc looked at me. Are you really
a policeman?

49
00:17:39,343 --> 00:17:47,472
I took the gold badge off my chain and put it in his good hand. Nigga,
I said, tonight I am whatever you need me to be.
